    "culture": {
      "text": {
        "id": 47,
        "title": "The Fox review – talking animals are the least weird thing in this delightfully peculiar comedy",
        "source": "The Guardian - Culture",
        "source_id": "guardian_culture",
        "category": "culture",
        "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/nov/26/the-fox-australian-movie-review-olivia-colman-sam-neill-dario-russo-emily-browning-jai-courtney",
        "published_at": "2026-11-25T14:00:29",
        "level": "C1",
        "slot_level": "C1",
        "source_level": "C1",
        "word_count": 679,
        "duration_sec": null,
        "body_text": "The vibe of writer-director Dario Russo’s delightfully peculiar feature debut is “messed-up fairytale”, pitched somewhere between the Brothers Grimm and Yorgos Lanthimos, with a distinctly Australian je ne sais quoi.",
        "audio_url": null,
        "description": null,
        "license": "proprietary",
        "attribution": null,
        "truncated": true,
        "guid_hash": "b989552f3f3593314f52fbdeffb04112fb656fce",
        "summary": "Dario Russo’s debut feature is Brothers Grimm meets Yorgos Lanthimos, with a standout cast including Olivia Colman, Sam Neill and Emily Browning The vibe of writer-director Dario Russo’s delightfully peculiar feature debut is “messed-up fairytale”, pitched somewhere between the Brothers Grimm and Yorgos Lanthimos, with a distinctly Australian je ne sais quoi . Russo is perhaps best known for co-creating the spy comedy series Danger 5, which had an aesthetic inspired by 60s and 70s men’s magazines; The Fox has a similarly thick retro texture. I love how this film feels as though it was made in consultation with the animal world, taking place in a universe where animals can speak to humans – though that’s perhaps the least weird thing about it.",
        "origin": "fresh",
        "badges": [],
        "periods": [],
        "themes": [],
        "history_terms": [],
        "alt_text": "The Fox review – talking animals are the least weird thing in this delightfully peculiar comedy — The Guardian - Culture",
